some things to consider:
1) nobaoby really wants to limit wishing to things you've discovered
    so far in the current game. that would clearly be no fun.

2) my wishing tree and the current wishing style need not be mutually
    exclusive (altho you could argue that they're not completely compatible).
    wishing could start with the prompt: "wish by name? [yn?]".

3) wishing does currently handle misspellings correctly. you get another
    chance. however the form of misspelling it botches is the name
    associated with special weapons. you can name a weapon (and other items)
    anything but only certain names have any meaning. this discussion was,
    in part, prompted by the fact that someone wished for a war hammer
    named Mjolnir and got it, not realizing that this is just a war hammer
    and not the special-case war hammer named Mjollnir.

4) a central issue in this is should the game depend on a player's ignorance
    for certain features? i think not, that's why wishing should be allowed
    to openly display all your possibilities. the game is strategically
    hard so even with a wish tree you would have to choose wisely. besides,
    there's no one secret way to win so what's to hide anyway? you can argue
    the thrill of discovery but it would take a lot of wishes to dampen that.
    besides, in any real context a player won't wish for an item that she/he
    doesn't feel will directly improve the chance of survival. few would wish
    for a magic flute just to possess one. and having seen it named doesn't
    dampen the thrill of discovering one altho it does let you know that
    such a thing exists whereas before you wouldn't have had a clue.

the most frightening thing about this may be the fact that it is new and
different ...

